From 3c81d1da77f6b98d93a2e396ff89e4d58d760ce9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Octo <180629317+OctoAlgo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 14:36:34 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] stages: the running mission's full stage list, read from
 memory; checkpoint #<n> counts in it

The title keeps a mission's stages as 32-byte heap objects {type, stage hash, 0, pointer, 0} and the
mission's stage list as a list object {type, data, count, capacity} whose data is an array of
pointers to those objects, in play order. No stage hash is known in advance: the stages the mission
has registered so far (patch 0013's table) are the anchors. Their objects are found by shape, then
every array of pointers to objects of the same type holding them, then each array's list object.
Exactly one list (by content) whose list object's count is the array's length, with the anchors in
registration order, must be found, or none is used. Our own search buffers are left out.

`stages [0x<script>]` prints the list with #numbers and marks the stages registered, announced and
jump tested. `checkpoint #<n>` counts in the full list and falls back to the registration table
when the list is not found. `memfind` / `memdump` stay as diagnostic commands for mining such
structures.

